<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<!--
	Verified empty by grepping this target's own source for every API on Apple's required-reason
	list (UserDefaults, file timestamps, disk space, active keyboard, system boot time) — none are
	used. purse-securefields-ios (the mandatory card-entry dependency) was checked the same way and
	is also clean, but ships no PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy of its own; PayPal and Klarna both bundle their
	own manifests inside their xcframeworks already, so nothing is owed on their behalf.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array/>
	<!--
	NOT YET FILLED IN — needs a privacy/legal decision, not an engineering guess: this SDK's
	monitoring collector (SDK-12265) sends device model, OS version and screen dimensions (see
	Checkout/Monitoring/CheckoutMonitoringContext.swift) alongside a self-generated, per-checkout
	instance id — never an IDFA/IDFV, never linked across apps. Whether that counts as
	NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeDeviceID / ...Diagnostics under Apple's taxonomy, and at what linkage
	level, is a categorization call for whoever signs off the App Store privacy nutrition label
	(compare against how the web and Android SDKs already disclose the same data). Leaving this
	array empty until that call is made would misrepresent real collection; do not ship this file
	as-is without that review.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>
</dict>
</plist>
